  "Modify or Extend Existing RTL": "For BCD arithmetic extension tasks, first confirm the expected top-level module/file actually exists in the staged RTL workspace before debugging logic.\nWhen upgrading a single-digit BCD block for chaining, add `cin`, remove legacy invalid-input handling, and keep the digit logic focused on raw-sum-plus-decimal-correction.\nImplement multi-digit subtraction with per-digit 9's-complement on `B` and an initial carry-in of `1`, then reuse the same chained BCD adder path for both add and subtract.\nFor subtraction-based comparators, treat the final carry-out as the no-borrow indicator, derive equality from an all-zero difference, and gate `greater` with `~equal`.\nPreserve filenames/module names and make the smallest compile-safe top/module assembly first when the harness error is an elaboration failure.",
  "Integrate multiple modules into a top module": "When composing fixed-latency crypto stages, align inter-stage start strobes to the proven pipeline-valid points from the original modules instead of adding extra wrapper latches that can shift outputs by a cycle.\nKeep the top-level done signal high while idle and drop it only on an accepted new start so burst attempts are naturally ignored while the pipeline is busy.\nRemove obsolete valid ports from reused submodules, but preserve their internal round pipeline structure and filenames to avoid breaking expected latency and elaboration.\nValidate integration first with any provided directed SV bench before rerunning the randomized cocotb harness; it exposes stage-order and cycle-alignment mistakes quickly.\nFor async FIFO integration, start by creating the exact expected top module and wire Gray-pointer synchronizers, pointer generators, and dual-port memory around a shared address-width parameter before debugging deeper behavior.",
  "Create new RTL from spec": "For asynchronous stack-style buffers built from spec, model depth from locally tracked push/pop progress and synchronized Gray-coded opposite-domain state so flags and top-of-stack addressing stay parameterized and compile-safe.\nFor UART transmitters built from spec, derive a single bit-tick counter from the baud divisor and keep TX/tready purely state-driven so AXIS handoff and UART framing stay cycle-aligned.\nFor UART RX spec harnesses, first create the exact expected top module and default parameters used by the testbench, then implement a cycle-counted FSM that samples start/data/parity/stop bits and emits a one-cycle AXIS-valid pulse.\nFor empty staged RTL on spec-driven harnesses, implement the exact top-level module first, then match cocotb-visible port names from the harness before refining logic.\nFor empty staged RTL on spec-driven RAM tasks, implement the exact top-level module and cocotb-visible ports first, then use a registered write stage with byte-wise merge logic so same-address dual-port collisions honor per-byte priority without breaking readback timing.",
  "Debug/fix buggy RTL": "When the harness samples outputs one cycle after internal progression updates, register `out_val` separately from the next-term accumulator and assert `done` only after the last value has been presented.\nIf cocotb accesses a 1-bit status port with `to_unsigned()`, expose it as a small packed vector while still driving only `0/1` values.\nWhen a broadcast AXIS block exposes a registered `s_axis_tready`, preserve the interface but add a small skid buffer so one in-flight beat accepted just before downstream backpressure is not overwritten or lost.\nWhen a simple combinational RTL block returns only `0/1` or all zeros under shift tests, first check for mistyped comparison operators in place of bit-shift operators before changing interfaces or control logic.\nWhen a Caesar-cipher RTL returns wrong case-preserving shifts, first verify the alphabetic range comparisons; inverted ASCII bound checks can misclassify letters while leaving the interface and arithmetic otherwise intact."
